steps=[
 step('Fix the actual experiment and standard matrix Borel structure','Retain the literal original product probability, physical rows, cosine features and inclusive local truncation. Define the deterministic mean as the actual Bochner integral of that same summand. The finite matrix Borel instance is fixed by a definition; probability and Borel compatibility are internal typing facts.',r'\mu=\gamma_d^{\otimes r},\qquad M_i=\int_\Omega Y_i\,d\mu,\qquad Z_i=Y_i-M_i.',pl,1,79),
 step('Transport row independence through the measurable truncated map','The canonical row law supplies measurability of each physical row, and the canonical joint row law supplies independence of the whole row family. The closed squared-radius event is measurable and every tensor coordinate is a continuous polynomial in the row coordinates. Subtracting a fixed actual mean gives an index-specific measurable map; joint independence is preserved under these separate maps.',r'F_i(x)=d^{-1}(xx^\top\otimes t_it_i^\top)\mathbf1_{\{\|x\|^2\le L\}},\quad Z_i=F_i(\Phi_i)-M_i,\quad(\Phi_i)_i\text{ independent}\Longrightarrow(Z_i)_i\text{ independent}.',pl,80,113),
 step('Use the actual integrable mean and prove pointwise symmetry','The existing actual-mean theorem gives genuine integrability of Yi and its radial formula. Real outer products and their tensor product are symmetric on the retained branch; outside it Yi is zero. The evaluated mean is also symmetric, so centering preserves self-adjointness. This chooses the radial-mean route; a separate direct integral-symmetry route is not an extra requirement.',r'a=\frac1r\int_{\mathbb R^r}\|x\|^2\mathbf1_{\{\|x\|^2\le L\}}\,d\gamma_r,\qquad M_i=\frac ad(I_r\otimes t_it_i^\top),\qquad Y_i^\top=Y_i,\quad M_i^\top=M_i.',pl,114,138),
 step('Prove true zero mean under the original probability','Subtracting a constant from the already integrable Yi proves integrability of Zi. Since the original measure is a probability, the integral of the constant Mi is Mi. Bochner linearity therefore gives the actual zero-mean identity. Combine this with measurability, joint independence and pointwise symmetry.',r'\int_\Omega Z_i\,d\mu=\int_\Omega Y_i\,d\mu-\mu(\Omega)M_i=M_i-M_i=0,\qquad Z_i(\omega)^\top=Z_i(\omega).',pl,139,148),
 step('Consume all centered properties for the weighted family','The complete private theorem retains the identical experiment, actual mean and cutoff, and calls the public theorem. It defines the literal deterministic source frequency and weights. Measurability is preserved by fixed real scalar multiplication; the remaining four assertions use their respective public conjuncts in the next step.',r'w_i=\cos((2q+1)\theta_i),\qquad X_i=w_iZ_i.',tl,1,93),
 step('Preserve independence, integrability, zero mean and symmetry','Apply joint independence preservation to the index-specific measurable maps A↦wiA. Fixed scalar multiplication preserves Bochner integrability and commutes with its integral, and real scalars preserve self-adjointness. None of these steps requires nonzero or positive weights. The two families are treated separately; no independence of their union is asserted.',r'(X_i)_i\text{ independent},\qquad\mathbb E_\mu X_i=w_i\mathbb E_\mu Z_i=0,\qquad X_i(\omega)^\top=w_iZ_i(\omega)^\top=X_i(\omega).',tl,94,108)
]
